{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Exact","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Quotient.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":16,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.quotEquivOfSurjective","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.quotEquivOfSurjective","kind":"def","line":181,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LongExactSequence.lean#L181-L189","doc":"**The quotient description.** Where `f_{n+1}` is onto, its target is the\nmiddle modulo the previous image — `M_{n+2} ≅ M_{n+1} ⧸ im f_n`.\n\nStated because vanishing is not always available: when the sequence does\nnot degenerate, this is what an exact spot still gives you. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.equivOfFlankingTermsZero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.equivOfFlankingTermsZero","kind":"def","line":167,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LongExactSequence.lean#L167-L179","doc":"**The classic pattern**, from vanishing *terms* rather than vanishing maps:\n\n    ⋯ → 0 → M n → M (n+1) → 0 → ⋯\n\nflanked by zeros on both sides, the middle map is an isomorphism. This is\nthe form a computation actually presents — vanishing results are statements\nabout groups, not about maps. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.equivOfNeighboursZero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.equivOfNeighboursZero","kind":"def","line":161,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LongExactSequence.lean#L161-L165","doc":"The isomorphism `bijective_of_neighbours_zero` provides. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.bijective_of_neighbours_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.bijective_of_neighbours_zero","kind":"theorem","line":143,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LongExactSequence.lean#L143-L159","doc":"**The theorem that closes a computation.**\n\nIf the maps on both sides vanish, `f_n` is an isomorphism — so an unknown\nmodule is pinned to a known one by two vanishing statements, neither of\nwhich mentions it. This is what an LES is *for*, and it is the shape every\n\"the obstruction vanishes, therefore the two agree\" argument in the corpus\nwants underneath it.\n\nThe predecessor index is carried as an equation rather than written `n-1`,\nbecause `n - 1 + 1` is only propositionally `n` in `ℤ` and the difference\nis a type mismatch on `M`. `subst` then discharges the transport. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.surjective_of_subsingleton_target","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.surjective_of_subsingleton_target","kind":"theorem","line":130,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LongExactSequence.lean#L130-L141","doc":"A vanishing term two steps along makes `f_n` surjective.\n\nThe target, not the source: `f_n` is onto when `f_{n+1}` is zero\n(`surjective_iff_next_eq_zero`), and what makes `f_{n+1}` zero without\nalso collapsing `f_n`'s own target is `M_{n+2}` vanishing. Deriving it\nfrom `Subsingleton (M (n+1))` instead would be true and useless — that\nhypothesis makes `f_n`'s codomain trivial, so surjectivity says nothing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.injective_of_subsingleton","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.injective_of_subsingleton","kind":"theorem","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LongExactSequence.lean#L125-L128","doc":"A vanishing term makes the map **out of its successor position** injective. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.map_eq_zero_of_subsingleton_target","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.map_eq_zero_of_subsingleton_target","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LongExactSequence.lean#L118-L123","doc":"A map **into** a zero module is zero. The companion to the above: a\nvanishing term kills the map arriving at it as well as the one leaving. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.map_eq_zero_of_subsingleton","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.map_eq_zero_of_subsingleton","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LongExactSequence.lean#L109-L116","doc":"A zero module has only the zero map out of it. Trivial, and it is the\ninput the theorems above are shaped for: it converts \"this group vanishes\"\n— the form vanishing results are always stated in — into the \"this map\nvanishes\" form exactness consumes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.injective_iff_prev_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.injective_iff_prev_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LongExactSequence.lean#L103-L107","doc":"**Injectivity is a statement about the previous map.** Dual to the above,\nand the reason a vanishing term propagates in both directions. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.surjective_iff_next_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.surjective_iff_next_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LongExactSequence.lean#L94-L101","doc":"**Surjectivity is a statement about the next map.**\n\n`f_n` is onto exactly when `f_{n+1}` is zero. Read right to left this is\nthe workhorse: prove something vanishes one step along, and you have\nsurjectivity here for free. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.comp_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.comp_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LongExactSequence.lean#L84-L92","doc":"Consecutive maps compose to zero — the half of exactness that always\nholds, recorded because it is what most calculations touch first. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.ker_eq_range","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.ker_eq_range","kind":"theorem","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LongExactSequence.lean#L79-L82","doc":"`ker f_{n+1} = range f_n`, in submodule form. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.LES.exact","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.LES.exact","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LongExactSequence.lean#L75-L75","doc":"Exact at every degree: `ker f_{n+1} = range f_n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.LES.map","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.LES.map","kind":"def","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LongExactSequence.lean#L73-L73","doc":"The map out of degree `n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.LES.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.LES.mk","kind":"ctor","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LongExactSequence.lean#L70-L70","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.LES","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LongExactSequence.LES","kind":"structure","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LongExactSequence.lean#L61-L75","doc":"**A long exact sequence**: a `ℤ`-indexed family of modules with maps\n`f_n : M n → M (n+1)`, exact at every spot.\n\nIndexed by `ℤ` rather than `ℕ` because the sequences this corpus produces\nare two-sided — a connecting map runs *down* in degree — and truncating at\nzero would put a spurious boundary where the mathematics has none. A\nbounded sequence is expressed by making the outlying terms subsingletons,\nwhich `map_eq_zero_of_subsingleton` turns into exactly the vanishing\nhypotheses the theorems below want. ","signatureChunk":0}]}